{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1120,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Sun and the Moon are about the same size\nwhen you look at them in the sky, though that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4890,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just thanks to the coincidence that the Sun\nis about 400 times farther away than the Moon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9000,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also about 400 times bigger.\nAnother fun coincidence is that the radius of the Sun"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13620,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is about twice the distance to the moon, so\nif you put the sun where the Earth is,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17300,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon would be just over halfway to the surface\nof the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20640,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Sun is also so big that it takes light\nover 4 and a half seconds to travel across"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24930,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its face – though I don't know whether that\nsays more about how big the Sun is, or how"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28480,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slow light is, or both."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30590,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What about the scale of other solar system\nobjects? Well, here are Pluto and Charon compared"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35219,
    "dDurationMs": 1090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Earth and the Moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36309,
    "dDurationMs": 4601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Jupiter; which, as a rough rule of thumb,\nhas about 10 times the radius of the earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40910,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and one tenth the radius of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43480,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saturn is slightly smaller than Jupiter, though\nits rings go out twice as far – still only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48260,
    "dDurationMs": 2049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a third of the way to the Moon, though."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50309,
    "dDurationMs": 6111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mars’ moons are very close and very small,\nwhich gives rise to another moon-sized coincidence:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56420,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Phobos is almost the same size in the martian\nsky as the Sun, and can cause annular eclipses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61670,
    "dDurationMs": 2009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like our Moon here on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63679,
    "dDurationMs": 4541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If two objects have the same apparent size\nin the sky, like the Moon and the Sun, it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68220,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means that their radii divided by their distances\nare about the same value. This also implies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73250,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the strengths of the objects’ tidal\nforces, which are roughly proportional to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76780,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the radius over the distance cubed, will also\nbe about the same. In fact, ANY two objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81440,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of roughly the same density that look about\nthe same size in the sky will have tidal forces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85780,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of similar strengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87340,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So while the apparent equal size of the moon\nand sun in our sky is a coincidence, the fact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92180,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they have similar apparent sizes AND\nroughly similar effects on the tides is a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96450,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consequence - \nof geometry\nand physics."
    } ]
  } ]
}
